.TH LIBESDM_ES_EXT 3 "2026-10-16" "ESDM @VERSION@" "ESDM Manual"
.SH NAME
libesdm_es_ext \- external entropy sources for the ESDM
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
.B #include <esdm/esdm_es_ext.h>
.PP
.B pkg\-config \-\-cflags \-\-libs esdm_es_ext
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
An external entropy source is noise source code that is not part of the ESDM.
It is written once, as a
.B struct esdm_es_ext_plugin
of the header
.IR esdm_es_ext.h ,
and handed to
.BR esdm\-server (8)
in one of two ways:
.TP
.B As a plugin library
A shared library exporting the struct under the name
.B esdm_es_ext_plugin
.RB ( ESDM_ES_EXT_PLUGIN_SYMBOL ).
The ESDM loads it into its own process and calls it directly; it is named
with
.B esdm\-server \-\-es_ext_lib
.I PATH
or the meson option
.BR es_ext_lib_path .
Building such a plugin needs the header only, not this library.
.TP
.B As a provider process
A process of its own that serves the plugin on a UNIX domain socket with the
provider server of this library. The ESDM connects to it; the socket is named
with
.B esdm\-server \-\-es_ext_socket
.I PATH
or the meson option
.BR es_ext_socket_path .
.BR esdm\-es\-ext\-serve (8)
is such a provider for a plugin library, so the same plugin runs either way.
.PP
A plugin in the ESDM runs with the privileges and in the namespaces of the
daemon and takes it down when it crashes; a provider keeps its own privileges,
its own address space and its own life cycle, at the cost of a round trip per
seed.
.PP
An ESDM may be built with several sources of each kind (meson options
.B es_ext_lib_num
and
.BR es_ext_socket_num ,
one each by default): each is an entropy source of its own, named
.BR ExternalLib0 ,
.BR ExternalLib1 ,
\&...
.BR ExternalSocket0 ,
\&... in the status of the ESDM \- or
.B ExternalLib
and
.B ExternalSocket
as the one source of its kind \- with its own plugin or provider, entropy rate
and state. A plugin library serves one source, as its callbacks are never
called concurrently; a provider process serves one plugin, and several
providers serve on sockets of their own.
.SS The plugin
.nf
struct esdm_es_ext_plugin {
	uint32_t version;
	const char *name;
	int (*init)(void **ctx);
	void (*fini)(void *ctx);
	int (*get_entropy)(void *ctx, uint8_t *buf, size_t buflen,
			   uint32_t *entropy_bits);
	int (*selftest)(void *ctx);
	/* Version 2 */
	int (*status_json)(void *ctx, char *buf, size_t buflen);
	/* Version 3 */
	uint32_t (*entropy_rate)(void *ctx);
};
.fi
.PP
.I version
is
.BR ESDM_ES_EXT_PLUGIN_VERSION ,
3. A plugin of version 1 or 2, whose struct ends before
.I status_json
or
.IR entropy_rate ,
is loaded all the same.
.PP
.I name
is the name of the source: the ESDM shows the source under it in its status
\- the
.B name
member of the source \- and in its log, in place of the name of the entropy
source the plugin is loaded as
.RB ( ExternalLib ,
.B ExternalLib1
and so on), which the status keeps as the
.B instance
of the source. It is 1 to
.B ESDM_ES_EXT_NAME_MAX
(32) bytes of UTF-8 without control characters \- no C0, DEL or C1 code point
\- terminated by a NUL. A name that is none of that, or the name of another
entropy source of the ESDM, is refused, not cut to size, and the source shown
under its instance name, as is a plugin whose
.I name
is NULL. Two sources may declare the same name; their instances tell them
apart. The ESDM takes the name when it initializes the plugin, after
.IR init .
.I get_entropy
fills all
.I buflen
bytes of
.I buf
and sets
.I *entropy_bits
to the entropy they hold, at most
.IR buflen " * 8,"
returning 0 \- or a negative errno if the source cannot deliver, upon which
the data is discarded. It must not block for long, as a DRNG waits for the
seed it is part of. The other callbacks may be NULL;
.I init
returns the context handed to the others,
.I selftest
checks the source without consuming the data it delivers. It runs before the
ESDM credits the source for the first time \- at start up before the ESDM
seeds its DRNGs, for a provider that is not there then on the first connection
to it \- and on the interval of the ESDM self tests.
.PP
.I status_json
writes the status of the source \- whatever helps whoever looks at the ESDM
status, such as the device read or the health of the noise source \- into
.I buf
as one JSON object terminated by a NUL, at most
.B ESDM_ES_EXT_STATUS_MAX
= 512 bytes of text, for which
.I buflen
leaves room. It returns 0,
.B \-EMSGSIZE
if the status does not fit, or another negative errno. The ESDM shows the
object as the
.B provider
member of the state of the source, in
.BR esdm_status_json ()
and as a section in the text of
.BR esdm_status (),
and leaves out a status that is no well-formed JSON object of up to 512 bytes
and 8 levels of nesting. It is asked for when the ESDM status is, at most
every 5 seconds \- the last status obtained shown in between \- and not while
a request of the ESDM to the source is in progress, the ESDM status going
without it then. It must not block for long: the source waits for it.
.PP
The callbacks are never called concurrently. The ESDM calls
.I init
while it still has its privileges, so that a device the source needs is opened
there \- but
.BR esdm\-server (8)
has left the mount, cgroup and network namespaces of the host by then already:
.I init
sees the file system of the host and can connect to a UNIX domain socket bound
to a path, but has no network \- no TCP or UDP endpoint, no abstract UNIX
domain socket of the host. The other callbacks run in the same namespaces,
after the ESDM dropped its privileges. A source that needs the network is
served by a provider instead: the provider server calls all callbacks in its
own process, with the privileges and in the namespaces it was started with.
.SS Entropy credit
The ESDM credits a block with the entropy the source claims for it, but at
most with what the entropy rate of the source allows: the rate configured for
the source
.RB ( es_ext_lib_entropy_rate ,
.BR es_ext_socket_entropy_rate ,
the corresponding
.BR esdm\-server (8)
options, or
.BR esdm_config_es_ext_lib_entropy_rate_set ()
and
.BR esdm_config_es_ext_socket_entropy_rate_set ()
of libesdm), else the rate the source declares itself, else a default \- 256
for a plugin library, which takes its claims as they are, 0 for a provider, so
that a provider declaring none is credited only once a rate is configured. The
status of the source shows the rate as
.B entropy_rate
and where it comes from as
.B entropy_rate_source
.RB ( config ,
.BR plugin ,
.B provider
or
.BR default ).
.PP
A plugin declares its rate with
.IR entropy_rate ,
bits of entropy per 256 bits of data from 0 to
.B ESDM_ES_EXT_ENTROPY_RATE_MAX
(256); a rate beyond is taken as 256. It is called once after
.IR init ,
so that the plugin can decide from the hardware it found, and only for a plugin
of version 3 or later. A provider declares its rate in its answer to INFO, see
below; the provider server of this library declares the one of the
.I entropy_rate
of its plugin, or the one set with
.BR esdm_es_ext_server_set_entropy_rate ().
A provider is another process, trusted for the user it runs as: accepting that
user
.RB ( "esdm\-server \-\-es_ext_socket_uid" )
means trusting the rate the provider declares as well. An operator who does not
want that configures the rate of the source, which takes precedence. A claim beyond the size of the block is a failure: the
block is discarded, and the source is credited with nothing until it delivers
properly again \- the ESDM asks a failed source again on its own to find out.
A source is credited with nothing before its first
.IR selftest ,
and a source failing it with nothing until a self test passes.
.SH FUNCTIONS
.SS Provider server
.TP
.BI "int esdm_es_ext_server_alloc(const struct esdm_es_ext_plugin *" plugin ", const char *" socket_path ", mode_t " mode ", struct esdm_es_ext_server **" server );
Create the
.B SOCK_SEQPACKET
socket file
.I socket_path
with exactly the access mode
.I mode
\- whatever the umask \- and call the
.I init
callback of
.IR plugin .
A socket file left at the path by an earlier instance is replaced, any other
file is not
.RB ( \-EEXIST ).
The ESDM connects with its privileges dropped (user
.B nobody
by default), so the mode has to let that user connect.
.TP
.BI "int esdm_es_ext_server_allow_uid(struct esdm_es_ext_server *" server ", uid_t " uid );
Serve only clients running as root or as an allowed user, up to 8 of them;
any other client is disconnected before it can take one of the few client
slots of the server, or drain the noise source. Without a user allowed, any
client that can connect is served. Allow the user the ESDM runs as: it
connects as root at start up, but with its privileges dropped once it
reconnects.
.TP
.BI "int esdm_es_ext_server_set_name(struct esdm_es_ext_server *" server ", const char *" name );
Declare
.I name
as the name of the source in the answer to INFO, in place of the
.I name
of the plugin, which the server declares by default \- if it is a valid one.
Returns
.B \-EINVAL
for a name that is not valid; NULL goes back to the name of the plugin. Call
it before
.BR esdm_es_ext_server_run ().
.TP
.BI "int esdm_es_ext_server_set_entropy_rate(struct esdm_es_ext_server *" server ", uint32_t " rate );
Declare
.I rate
bits of entropy per 256 bits of data, 0 to 256, as the rate of the source in
the answer to INFO, in place of the one the
.I entropy_rate
callback of the plugin returns, which the server declares by default \- none
for a plugin without it. Returns
.B \-EINVAL
for a rate beyond 256;
.B ESDM_ES_EXT_PROTO_RATE_NONE
goes back to the rate of the plugin. Call it before
.BR esdm_es_ext_server_run ().
.TP
.BI "int esdm_es_ext_server_set_dtls(struct esdm_es_ext_server *" server ", int " enable );
Offer DTLS to the clients, or not. A library built with DTLS offers it by
default; enabling it in one built without returns
.BR \-EOPNOTSUPP .
.TP
.BI "int esdm_es_ext_server_run(struct esdm_es_ext_server *" server );
Serve the connected clients, one request after the other, until
.BR esdm_es_ext_server_stop ()
is called. A client that breaks the protocol is disconnected. Up to 8 clients
are served at a time: when another one connects, the client that sent nothing
for the longest time is disconnected to make room for it \- one running as
root only for another one running as root, as the ESDM connects as root at
start up. Clients holding their slots without using them so cannot lock the
ESDM out, which connects again at once when it finds its connection closed.
.TP
.BI "void esdm_es_ext_server_stop(struct esdm_es_ext_server *" server );
Make
.BR esdm_es_ext_server_run ()
return. Callable from another thread and from a signal handler.
.TP
.BI "void esdm_es_ext_server_free(struct esdm_es_ext_server *" server );
Close the connections, remove the socket file \- the one created, not one
another instance put there meanwhile \- and call the
.I fini
callback of the plugin.
.SS Plugin loader
.TP
.BI "int esdm_es_ext_plugin_load(const char *" path ", struct esdm_es_ext_plugin_handle **" handle ", const struct esdm_es_ext_plugin **" plugin );
Load the plugin library
.I path
and check the plugin it exports, without calling its
.I init
callback \- the plugin is meant for
.BR esdm_es_ext_server_alloc (),
which does. Returns
.B \-ENOENT
for a library that cannot be loaded,
.B \-ENOEXEC
for one exporting no plugin, and
.B \-EPROTO
for a plugin of a version beyond
.B ESDM_ES_EXT_PLUGIN_VERSION
or without
.IR get_entropy .
.TP
.BI "void esdm_es_ext_plugin_unload(struct esdm_es_ext_plugin_handle *" handle );
Unload the library.
.SH RETURN VALUE
The functions returning an int return 0 on success and a negative errno on
failure.
.SH PROTOCOL
The ESDM sends requests on its connection to the provider one at a time; the
provider answers each with exactly one response. Requests and responses are
one
.B SOCK_SEQPACKET
message each, all integers little endian.
.PP
A request is 12 bytes: the magic
.B ESDM_ES_EXT_PROTO_MAGIC
(the bytes "ESEX"), the version
.B ESDM_ES_EXT_PROTO_VERSION
(u16, 1), the type (u16:
.B ESDM_ES_EXT_PROTO_GET_ENTROPY
= 1,
.B ESDM_ES_EXT_PROTO_SELFTEST
= 2,
.B ESDM_ES_EXT_PROTO_STATUS
= 4 or
.B ESDM_ES_EXT_PROTO_INFO
= 5) and the length (u32: the bytes asked for, 1 to
.B ESDM_ES_EXT_PROTO_MAX_DATA
= 512, for GET_ENTROPY; the largest status taken, 1 to
.B ESDM_ES_EXT_STATUS_MAX
= 512, for STATUS; 0 for SELFTEST and INFO).
.PP
A response is 16 bytes: magic, version and type as in the request, the status
(s32: 0 or a negative errno) and the entropy of the data (u32, at most 8 times
the length for GET_ENTROPY, 0 otherwise); a GET_ENTROPY answered with status 0
is followed by exactly the length bytes of data, a STATUS answered with status
0 by the status of the source \- the JSON text of
.IR status_json ,
1 to length bytes without the NUL \- an INFO answered with status 0 by what
the provider declares of itself, see below, and any other response by
nothing.
.PP
A message that breaks any of this ends the connection, and so does a request
of a type the provider does not know.
.SS STATUS
The ESDM asks the provider for its status when its own status is asked for,
at most every 5 seconds, and not while another request is in progress. A
provider whose plugin has
no
.I status_json
answers
.BR \-EOPNOTSUPP ,
one whose status does not fit into the length
.BR \-EMSGSIZE .
The ESDM checks that the status is a well-formed JSON object before it shows
it. A provider older than STATUS closes the connection on it, as on any
request it does not know: the ESDM connects again at once, still crediting the
provider, and does not ask again until it connects the next time. A connection
found closed when STATUS is sent, as one to a provider restarted meanwhile, is
no such refusal: the ESDM connects again and asks on the new connection. A
provider not answering STATUS within the timeout of a request is disconnected
as for any request, connected to again after the retry interval, and not asked
for its status for a minute.
.SS INFO
The ESDM asks the provider what it declares of itself once per connection,
before any other request but STARTDTLS: inside the DTLS session where there is
one. The request has length 0. A provider answers it with status 0, entropy 0
and the data
.PP
.nf
	u32 entropy_rate	0 to 256, or 0xffffffff for none
	u8  name[]		0 to 32 bytes
.fi
.PP
\- 4 to 36 bytes \- where
.I entropy_rate
is the rate the provider declares for its data, bits of entropy per 256 bits,
.B ESDM_ES_EXT_PROTO_RATE_NONE
(0xffffffff) for none, and
.I name
is the name of the source without a NUL, no bytes for a provider without a
name; or with a negative errno, such as
.BR \-EOPNOTSUPP ,
and no data, which declares nothing. The ESDM shows the source under the name
as a plugin library under the
.I name
of its plugin, and takes it only if it is valid in the same way; a name that is
not valid is not taken, and the source shown under its instance name. It
credits the provider at the rate unless a rate is configured for the source,
taking a rate beyond 256 as 256. An answer of more than 36 bytes, or another
answer that breaks the protocol, ends the connection as for any request. The
provider server of this library answers with the
.I name
and the
.I entropy_rate
of its plugin, or the ones set with
.BR esdm_es_ext_server_set_name ()
and
.BR esdm_es_ext_server_set_entropy_rate ().
A provider older than INFO closes the connection on it, as on any request it
does not know: the ESDM connects again at once without asking on the new
connection, and uses the provider without a name and a rate. Both are asked
for anew on every connection \- a provider restarted may declare others.
.SS DTLS
An ESDM built with DTLS (meson option
.BR dtls ,
with the TLS stack of its crypto backend) sends
.B ESDM_ES_EXT_PROTO_STARTDTLS
(type 3, length 0) as its first request. A provider answering it with status 0
expects a DTLS 1.2 handshake on the connection next, the ESDM being the client.
Every DTLS record is one message of the socket; a request or response travels
as DTLS application data, in records carrying one byte in front of their part
of it \- 1 if more parts follow, 0 for the last one. A provider that cannot do
DTLS answers
.BR \-EOPNOTSUPP ,
and the connection goes on unencrypted; one that does not know STARTDTLS closes
the connection, and the ESDM connects again without asking.
.PP
The DTLS is opportunistic and unauthenticated: the provider presents an
ephemeral self-signed certificate that is not verified. What the ESDM trusts
the provider for remains the credentials of the process on the other end of
the socket; the DTLS keeps the data from being read off the channel.
.SH EXAMPLE
A plugin library, built with
.B cc \-shared \-fPIC
and loaded with
.BR "esdm\-server \-\-es_ext_lib /usr/lib/esdm/my_es.so" ,
or served with
.BR "esdm\-es\-ext\-serve \-\-plugin /usr/lib/esdm/my_es.so \-\-socket /run/my\-es.socket" :
.PP
.nf
#include <esdm/esdm_es_ext.h>

static int my_get_entropy(void *ctx, uint8_t *buf, size_t buflen,
			  uint32_t *entropy_bits)
{
	(void)ctx;

	if (read_my_noise_source(buf, buflen))
		return \-EIO;

	/* The entropy assessment of the noise source: 1 bit per byte */
	*entropy_bits = buflen;
	return 0;
}

ESDM_ES_EXT_PLUGIN_EXPORT
const struct esdm_es_ext_plugin esdm_es_ext_plugin = {
	.version = ESDM_ES_EXT_PLUGIN_VERSION,
	.name = "my-noise-source",
	.get_entropy = my_get_entropy,
};
.fi
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR esdm\-server (8),
.BR esdm\-es\-ext\-serve (8),
.BR libesdm (3)
.SH AUTHOR
Markus Theil <theil.markus@gmail.com>
